nodes.body:
Rebased onto current main. The bug is still present there.

**`latency - ttft == sum(itl)` holds.** A first token-bearing chunk can carry more than one token. Its extra tokens are recorded as zero-length intervals, keeping the count at `output_tokens - 1` without charging prefill time to inter-token latency. 300 of 300 requests satisfy the identity, maximum residual 0.

**TTFT moves later for streams that open with a role-only chunk.** Main sets TTFT on any chunk with a non-empty `choices`. This branch waits for content, reasoning or a tool call. Measured on Qwen2.5-0.5B, 271 ms here against 235 ms on main. That same chunk also fabricates an interval on main: 108 of 300 requests recorded 256 ITL samples for 256 output tokens.

`benchmarks/backend_request_func.py` carries a second copy of this client with the same bug, so the change is applied there too. #55508 kept both in sync and this follows that.

Evidence is in the description: 8 unit tests, the 18 upstream timing tests untouched and green, and end-to-end runs on both copies. For 300 requests of 256 output tokens this branch records exactly 76,500 intervals, which is 300 x 255. On main the packaged copy ranged 74,339 to 76,544 across three runs, and the `benchmarks/` copy lost intervals on every one of the 300 requests.

@bbrowning you filed #27485 and the stale bot closed it on 16 June. The bug is still on main, with numbers in the description. Could you reopen it?

@surajm20061998 this keeps the `latency - ttft == sum(itl)` identity from #55508, checked across 300 requests at concurrency 64 on both clients. If you have time to look, that would help.

@taneem-ibrahim this touches the `first_chunk_received` guard you added in #54708. If you have time to look, that would help.

